<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">2 February in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 2 February, from 506 to 2007.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">506</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alaric II issues the Breviary of Alaric</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Visigothic king promulgated a digest of Roman law for the Roman population of his kingdom in Gaul and Spain. It became one of the main channels through which Roman law reached early medieval western Europe.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1536</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pedro de Mendoza founds Buenos Aires</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Spanish expedition founded a settlement on the Río de la Plata. Hunger and attacks forced its abandonment in 1541, and Juan de Garay refounded Buenos Aires in 1580.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1653</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">New Amsterdam receives municipal government</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Dutch settlement on Manhattan was granted its own city government under Director-General Peter Stuyvesant. The English seized it in 1664 and renamed it New York.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1848</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo ends the Mexican-American War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Mexico ceded about half its territory, including California, Nevada, Utah and most of Arizona and New Mexico, to the United States for 15 million dollars, and recognised Texas as American.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1943</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">German Sixth Army's last units surrender at Stalingrad</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The final German forces in Stalingrad's northern pocket laid down their arms. Some 91,000 were taken prisoner, and the defeat marked a turning point of the war on the Eastern Front.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1971</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Idi Amin proclaims himself President of Uganda</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A week after his coup, Amin declared himself head of state and suspended parts of the constitution. His rule became one of the most brutal in post-colonial Africa until his overthrow in 1979.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1990</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">F. W. de Klerk lifts the ban on the ANC</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Opening parliament in Cape Town, South Africa's President legalised the African National Congress and other banned groups and announced Nelson Mandela's release. Negotiations to end apartheid followed.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">2007</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">IPCC finds global warming is 'very likely' caused by humans</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Released in Paris, the summary of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change's fourth assessment called warming 'unequivocal' and very likely due to human emissions. The panel shared that year's Nobel Peace Prize.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></li></ol>
